# scenarios/2_vat.books: loaded by ledger_server into 2_vat.db the first time it is picked,
# and again by Start over. Amounts are in cents.
title	2. VAT: collected for the tax office, not earned
about	The VAT paid on a purchase is claimed back (VAT receivable), the VAT charged on a sale is owed (VAT payable); neither is a cost or a revenue. At the VAT return the two are set off, and the bank pays the difference.
about	Try it: record a purchase with 21% VAT, and see where the VAT goes.
currency	USD
account	100000	equity	Share capital
account	550000	asset	Bank
account	240000	asset	Furniture and equipment
account	411000	asset	VAT receivable
account	700000	revenue	Sales
account	400000	asset	Trade receivables
account	451000	liability	VAT payable
entry	2011-01-02	The founder puts in savings
posting	Share capital	-1000000
posting	Bank	1000000
entry	2011-01-10	A desk for 1,000 (21% VAT)
posting	Bank	-121000
posting	Furniture and equipment	100000
posting	VAT receivable	21000
entry	2011-02-01	Consulting invoiced to a customer for 2,000 (21% VAT)
posting	Sales	-200000
posting	VAT payable	-42000
posting	Trade receivables	242000
entry	2011-02-28	The customer pays, VAT included
posting	Trade receivables	-242000
posting	Bank	242000
entry	2011-04-20	The VAT return: 420 owed less 210 to recover, the bank pays 210
posting	VAT receivable	-21000
posting	Bank	-21000
posting	VAT payable	42000
